```verilog

module Mult4to1 (In1, In2, In3, In4, Sel, Out);

    input [31:0] In1, In2, In3, In4; //four 32-bit inputs

    input [1:0] Sel; //selector signal

    output reg [31:0] Out;// 32-bit output

    always @(In1, In2, In3, In4, Sel)

    case (Sel) //a 4->1 multiplexor

        0: Out <= In1;

        1: Out <= In2;

        2: Out <= In3;

        default: Out <= In4;

    endcase

endmodule

FIGURE A.4.2 A Verilog definition of a 4-to-1 multiplexor with 32-bit inputs, using a case statement. The case statement acts like a C switch statement, except that in Verilog only the code associated with the selected case is executed (as if each case state had a break at the end) and there is no fall-through to the next statement.

```verilog

module MIPSALU (ALUctl, A, B, ALUOut, Zero);

    input [3:0] ALUctl;

    input [31:0] A,B;

    output reg [31:0] ALUOut;

    output Zero;

    assign Zero = (ALUOut==0); //Zero is true if ALUOut is 0; goes anywhere

    always @(ALUctl, A, B) //reevaluate if these change

        case (ALUctl)

            0: ALUOut <= A & B;

            1: ALUOut <= A | B;

            2: ALUOut <= A + B;

            6: ALUOut <= A - B;

            7: ALUOut <= A < B ? 1:0;

            12: ALUOut <= ~(A | B); // result is nor

            default: ALUOut <= 0; //default to 0, should not happen;

        endcase

endmodule

```

FIGURE A.4.3 A Verilog behavioral definition of a LEGv8 ALU. This could be synthesized using a module library containing basic arithmetic and logical operations.

FIGURE A.5.14 The symbol commonly used to represent an ALU, as shown in Figure A.5.12. This symbol is also used to represent an adder, so it is normally labeled either with ALU or Adder.

```verilog

module MIPSALU (ALUctl, A, B, ALUOut, Zero);

    input [3:0] ALUctl;

    input [63:0] A,B;

    output reg [63:0] ALUOut;

    output Zero;

    assign Zero = (ALUOut==0); //Zero is true if ALUOut is 0

    always @(ALUctl, A, B) begin //reevaluate if these change

        case (ALUctl)

            0: ALUOut <= A & B;

            1: ALUOut <= A | B;

            2: ALUOut <= A + B;

            6: ALUOut <= A - B;

            7: ALUOut <= A < B ? 1 : 0;

            12: ALUOut <= ~(A | B); // result is nor

            default: ALUOut <= 0;

        endcase

    end

endmodule

```

FIGURE A.5.15 A Verilog behavioral definition of a LEGv8 ALU.

$$
\begin{array}{l} c 4 = g 3 + (p 3 \cdot g 2) + (p 3 \cdot p 2 \cdot g 1) + (p 3 \cdot p 2 \cdot p 1 \cdot g 0) \\ + \left(p 3 \cdot p 2 \cdot p 1 \cdot p 0 \cdot c 0\right) \\ \end{array}
$$
